# TryHackMe: Linux Fundamentals Part 3 - System Maintenance & Log Analysis

This repository documents the core concepts learned during the TryHackMe Linux Fundamentals Part 3 room, focusing on system maintenance, package management, and real-world troubleshooting techniques for log tracking.

---

## 🛠️ Log Directory Troubleshooting Walkthrough

### The Scenario
When investigating server history files, running into a `Permission Denied` or an authentication error is common. This walkthrough demonstrates how to investigate the system independently when standard tutorial steps fail.

### 1. Inspecting File Properties
When a file blocks access, the first step is checking its owner and group boundaries:
```bash
ls -l /var/log/apache2/access.log
```
If the output shows strict ownership (e.g., owned by `root` and the `adm` group) and your current user (`id`) is not part of those groups, direct reading commands will be blocked by Linux.

### 2. Identifying Empty Active Logs & Log Rotation
If the active log file is readable but shows **0 bytes** of data, it means the system has recently archived the text. Running a directory discovery command reveals the full picture:
```bash
ls -la /var/log/apache2/
```
This reveals backup log files created by the system framework. In many lab environments or misconfigured environments, these backup files (e.g., `access.log.1`) may possess relaxed permissions, allowing standard users to read the historical traffic data directly without administrative escalation.

